    <encodingDesc>
      <classDecl>
        <!-- Taxonomies are separated by analytical level: genre/form, function/tone, topic/aboutness, occasion/context,
          and rhetorical/formal features. -->

        <!-- see terminology used in Richard Wills' Poematum liber (London, 1573) schematum (sigs Fiir-Fiiiv); translation at Hum library PN1040 WIL 1958 -->
        <!-- Wills also refers to Julius Caesar Scaliger Poetics, the massive encyclopedia of classical poetics [Poetices libri spetem] (Lyon, 1561; published posthumously; reprinted 1586, 1594; revised 1607, 1617). Played an important role in the emergency of modern literary criticism; constitutes an enormous thesaurus of poetic forms and figures; a taxonomy of poetic forms and their origins) -->

        <!-- vernacular poets writing in English adopted these devices from the Anglo-Latin poetry; this taxonomy help to trace the passage of these ubiquitous devises from Latin to vernacular poetry -->

        <!-- this is a controlled vocabulary; and works like this: <catRef target="../taxonomy/rup-taxonomy.xml#your-category"/> -->
        
        <!--INSTRUCTIONS FOR USE (RUP Taxonomy) 
          This taxonomy file (rup-taxonomy.xml) is stored in: /taxonomy/
        Individual poem TEI files are stored in: /poems/
        To reference a category from this taxonomy in a poem file, use <catRef> with a relative path: <catRef target="../taxonomy/rup-taxonomy.xml#CATEGORY_ID"/>
        Replace CATEGORY_ID with the xml:id of the relevant <category> in this file.

        Example:
        <category xml:id="political">
        <catDesc>Political content</catDesc>
          </category>
        In poem file:
        <catRef target="../taxonomy/rup-taxonomy.xml#political"/>

          Notes:
          - The path must match the folder structure exactly.
          - The xml:id values must be unique and consistent.
          - This taxonomy is shared across all poem files (do not duplicate it).
          - Update all catRef targets if the file is moved or renamed.-->

